cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  BehavioralDynamics: multiscale behavioral analysis of automated homecage RFID
  tracking data
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Tobias
    family-names: Pohl
    email: tobias.pohl@mdc-berlin.de
    affiliation: >-
      Max Delbrück Center for Molecular Medicine in the Helmholtz Association
      (MDC), Berlin, Germany; Hörnberg Lab
repository-code: 'https://github.com/topohl/BehavioralDynamics'
url: 'https://github.com/topohl/BehavioralDynamics'
abstract: >-
  An R analysis workflow for longitudinal homecage behavioral tracking data from
  automated RFID position systems. It moves from preprocessed RFID position data
  to manuscript-facing behavioral metrics, statistics, prospective prediction
  models and QC summaries, with an emphasis on the temporal organization of
  behavior rather than summary means alone. Analyses cover tracking-integrity QC,
  multiscale movement, entropy and co-location metrics, cage-change and circadian
  phase annotation, dyadic contact and social network features, temporal
  instability, behavioral state-space and optional hidden Markov state models,
  GAMM trajectory features, prospective early prediction of later stress burden,
  and optional behavior-proteomics integration. Developed for a mouse social
  instability stress experiment; several components are reusable, but the
  repository is not a general-purpose package.
keywords:
  - behavioral neuroscience
  - homecage monitoring
  - RFID tracking
  - mouse behavior
  - social instability stress
  - stress resilience
  - longitudinal behavioral phenotyping
  - hidden Markov model
  - reproducible research
  - R
